\section{Spatial-Narrowband Sufficient Condition}
\label{sec:squint_supp}

Section~\ref{main-sec:system_model} of the main paper defines the neglected
spatial phase $\epsilon_{\rm sq}$ in Section~\ref{main-subsec:defchain} and audits it
through the exact wavefront differences. The
following closed-form sufficient condition is the design-time version of
that audit. With fractional bandwidth $\beta_{\rm BW}=B_{\rm occ}/f_c$,
aperture radius $a_k$, UE range $R_k$, and in-plane direction component
$\bu_{k,\parallel}$, the center-frequency spatial-manifold model is
justified when
\begin{equation}
	\beta_{\rm BW}\left(
	\frac{a_k}{\lambda_c}\|\bu_{k,\parallel}\|_2
	+ \frac{a_k^2}{\lambda_c R_k}\right)\ll 1,
	\label{eq:squint_cond}
\end{equation}
whose second term is the fractional bandwidth times the Fresnel number
$R_{{\rm Fr},k}/R_k$ with the Fresnel-number length
$R_{{\rm Fr},k}=a_k^2/\lambda_c$, distinct from the Fraunhofer distance
$R_{\rm FF}$ of main-paper (A3). Near broadside the linear
aperture term is small and the curvature term dominates; away from
broadside the conventional squint term $\beta_{\rm BW}a_k/\lambda_c$ must
also be small. The same test applies separately to the inter-element path
differences of the RIS--BS and BS-ULA responses: knowing the infrastructure
geometry does not remove the frequency dependence of its spatial response.
Condition \eqref{eq:squint_cond} is a design screen and is not a substitute
for the exact audit, which must be evaluated through the exact wavefront
differences rather than inferred from the fractional bandwidth alone. In
the reference configuration ($\beta_{\rm BW}=0.52\%$) the audited values
are $0.102$, $0.058$ and $0.048$~rad, with maximum wavefront differences of
$31.3$, $17.8$ and $14.8$~mm. All reported results are generated under the same
center-frequency manifold used by the estimator and therefore do not
measure sensitivity to \eqref{eq:squint_cond}.

\section{Component Ablation at Both Operating Points}
\label{sec:ablation_supp}

Table~\ref{tab:s_ablation} gives the component ladder at $-10$ and
$-20$~dB; the main paper retains the acquisition metrics at both points.
At $-10$~dB, compression reduces $P_{\rm cat}$ from $2.92\%$ to $0/480$
trials (exact $95\%$ interval $[0,0.77]\%$), while basin acquisition rises
from $96.04\%$ to $99.17\%$ and reaches $100\%$ after common geometry.
Thus the compression benefit is observed inside the operating region.

At $-20$~dB the rungs separate more clearly: compression reduces
$P_{\rm cat}$ from $20.42\%$ to $6.04\%$, one-start geometry raises basin
capture to $93.96\%$, and four starts yield $4.17\%$ catastrophic events
and $95.21\%$ basin capture. Anchor refresh leaves both counts unchanged
while reducing reconstruction-NMSE p95 from $9.53\times10^{-2}$ to
$1.12\times10^{-3}$. Seven starts and an oracle-position start give
$3.54\%$ and $3.75\%$, respectively, compared with $4.17\%$ for the
proposed route. These controls do not exclude a small restart benefit.

Runtimes in Table~\ref{tab:s_ablation} are medians of the parallel harness
and are therefore throughput under contention, not the serialized cost of
one estimate; the serialized figures are in Section~\ref{main-subsec:res_cost} of the
main paper.

