\section{Resolvability Summary}
\label{sec:resolv_supp}

This section gives the full resolution table behind
Fig.~\ref{main-fig:c1_separability} and the separability claim of
Section~\ref{main-subsec:res_c1}. Each cell is $480$ paired trials at
$-10$~dB at one delay separation and one Jones overlap. A trial counts as
resolved when the following conditions hold jointly: the estimator does not fail, it
returns exactly $K$ components, every component is paired with the correct
physical panel, its largest Phase-I delay error is at most $0.5$~ns, and its
smallest estimated delay separation is at least $0.05$~ns. Position accuracy
is deliberately not part of the criterion, so the statistic measures
separation of the components themselves rather than the accuracy of the fix
built on them; a failure of any condition leaves the trial in the
denominator. Table~\ref{tab:s_resolv} reports the resolution probability
against overlap for the three receiver modes.

At $1$~ns separation, full-EVS resolution ranges from $0.860$ to $0.875$
over the four overlaps. At overlap $0.1$, the dual and scalar rates are
$0.0042$ and zero; at the other overlaps the dual rate is at least $0.508$.
Full-EVS resolution is about $0.95$ at $2$~ns and $0.53$ at $0.5$~ns.
At $0.1$~ns, no mode resolves more than three trials out of $480$.

The maximum overlap-dependent spreads are $1.46$, $82.92$ and $63.54$
percentage points for full, dual and scalar reception.
Table~\ref{tab:s_resolv} collects these spreads together with each mode's
weakest single cell.

