\emph{Colored noise.} The objective is unwhitened. At correlation
$\rho=0,0.5,0.8$, proposed catastrophic rates are $0.83\%,0.42\%,0.21\%$,
compared with $1.04\%,1.88\%,2.71\%$ for the raw-delay control.
Proposed conditional RMSE changes from $0.436$ to $0.310$~mm across
the sweep. Sufficiency under colored noise would require whitening, which is
not applied here.

\emph{Model order.} At $0$~dB, $\widehat K=2$ gives a $0.705$-m median
and $65.63\%$ catastrophic events. For $\widehat K=4$ and $5$, medians
remain $0.150$ and $0.299$~mm but catastrophic rates are $34.79\%$ and
$47.92\%$, with p95 errors $1.27$ and $1.73$~m. Thus small medians under
overestimation coexist with a large failure population. The model assumes
known panel count; these results do not support robust order selection.

\section{Estimator Pseudocode}
\label{sec:pseudocode_supp}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:s_mksc_ccop} collects the two phases developed in
Section~\ref{main-sec:algorithm} of the main paper. Equation numbers refer to
the main paper.

\begin{algorithm}[!tb]
\caption{MKSC-GI Initialized CCOP-JVP}
\label{alg:s_mksc_ccop}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE $\{\bY_t\}_{t=1}^{T}$, calibrated infrastructure, $\mathcal P$, $\mathcal T$, $K$
\REQUIRE constants $\lambda_{\rm rel},\lambda_{\rm fl},w_{\rm clk},
\varrho_0,\varpi_{\min},\{\lambda_k\},\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ as fixed in
Section~\ref{sec:impl_supp}
\STATE Build $\bQ_S$ from calibration; compress the receive index of each $\bY_t$.
\STATE Estimate $K$ poles from $\bY_f$; rebuild the Vandermonde design $\bD$.
\STATE Recover coupled factors by \eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short}; split and lift each column.
\STATE Assign columns to panels by \eqref{main-eq:assoc_score}--\eqref{main-eq:assoc_perm}.
\STATE Generate Rayleigh-spaced beam-space proposals by adjoint/Fresnel-dechirp 2-D transform; re-score by \eqref{main-eq:Jris_short}, the exact spherical-wave model, and refine.
\STATE Minimize scale-profiled $J_{\rm geom}$ from the four median-based starts.
\STATE Refresh Jones anchors by a fixed-position free-Jones clock profile.
\STATE Minimize $\widetilde F(\bp)$, certifying the inner clock solve at every evaluated position.
\RETURN $\hat\bp,\hat{\Delta t},\hat\bx$ and certificate diagnostics.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Implementation Specification}
\label{sec:impl_supp}

This section fixes the operational detail of
Section~\ref{main-sec:algorithm} that the main text states only by name. All
values are those of the frozen \texttt{paper\_v4} campaign, which builds its
configuration from the package defaults; where a call site carries a
different fallback constant, the campaign value below is the one that
applies.

\subsection{Array and Gauge Conventions}
\label{sec:coords_supp}

On an $M_{Rx}\times M_{Ry}$ grid of spacings $(d_x,d_y)$ the panel elements
are centered on the panel in its own frame,
\begin{equation}
	\brho_{k,m}=\left[\left(m_x-\tfrac{M_{Rx}-1}{2}\right)d_x,\;
	\left(m_y-\tfrac{M_{Ry}-1}{2}\right)d_y,\;0\right]^{\mathsf T},
	\label{eq:s_rho}
\end{equation}
and the BS uniform linear array lies along the global $x$ axis at
$x_i=(i-\tfrac{M_A-1}{2})d_A$ for $i=0,\ldots,M_A-1$, with
$\{\bm u_x,\bm u_y,\bm u_z\}$ the global axes and $\kappa=2\pi/\lambda_c$ the
center-frequency wavenumber.

The transverse basis of main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:qbasis} is built from
$\bm u_z$, which degenerates when the RIS-to-BS direction approaches the
pole. The implementation therefore replaces $\bm u_z$ by $\bm u_y$ whenever
$|\bs_{{\rm RB},k}^{\mathsf T}\bm u_z|>0.95$, which keeps the cross product
well conditioned. The substitution is a gauge choice and changes no
observable: any other transverse frame differs by a $2\times2$ orthogonal
factor that the unknown Jones state absorbs, so
$\bTheta_k^{(6)}$ changes only by right multiplication with that factor and
every quantity in Theorems~\ref{main-thm:split}--\ref{main-thm:clock} is
invariant.

Finally, the stacking that produces main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:Ak} is
read off one scalar sample. Pilots are unit modulus and removed by pilot
normalization, so with $y_{i,n,t}$ the sample at receive index $i$,
subcarrier $n$ and configuration $t$,
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
	y_{i,n,t}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}
	&\Big(\sum_{m=1}^{M_R}[\bOmega_k]_{t,m}[\bg_k]_m\Big)\\
	&\times e^{-j2\pi\left(n-\frac{N-1}{2}\right)\Delta f\tau_k}\,
	[\bBm_k]_{i,:}\bx_k+n_{i,n,t},
\end{split}
	\label{eq:s_scalar}
\end{equation}
with $\bg_k=\bg_k(\bp)$ as in main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:hk}. The inner
sum is the reflection at panel $k$, the $t$th entry of $\bh_k(\bp)$,
carrying both the spherical UE--RIS and plane-wave RIS--BS phases; the
exponential is the OFDM ramp of the cascaded delay; and
$[\bBm_k]_{i,:}\bx_k$ is the Maxwell--Jones response. All $K$ panels
reflect in every configuration, so their contributions add coherently, and
because \eqref{eq:s_scalar} separates in $t$, $n$ and $i$, ordering the
samples with the EVS index fastest and stacking reproduces main-paper
Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:Ak}, while reading $i$ down the rows and $n$ across the
columns at fixed $t$ gives main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:obsmat}.

\subsection{Frequency Smoothing and Delay Poles}

Pole estimation runs on the compressed frequency matrix
$\bY_f\in\C^{N\times rT}$ of main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:freqsnap}, which
treats the compressed receive and training indices as snapshots. It is
augmented in forward--backward form
$\bY_{\rm aug}=[\bY_f,\ \bJ\bY_f^{\ast}]$ with $\bJ$ the exchange matrix.
The $K$ leading left singular vectors of $\bY_{\rm aug}$ give the signal
subspace $\bE_s$; the shift-invariant pair is $\bE_1=\bE_s(1{:}N-1,:)$ and
$\bE_2=\bE_s(2{:}N,:)$, and the rotation is taken by total least squares,
$\bPsi_{\rm TLS}=-\bV_{12}\bV_{22}^{-1}$ from the right singular vectors of
$[\bE_1,\bE_2]$, falling back to ordinary least squares when
$N-1<2K$. The poles are the eigenvalues of $\bPsi_{\rm TLS}$, each divided
by its modulus so that only the delay phase survives. The shift invariance
is therefore read on the tone index itself, over the full $N$-tone
aperture, and no smoothing window enters the pole estimate.

The smoothing weights of main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} use
the balanced window $P=\lceil N/2\rceil$, $L=N+1-P$, so that
$w_n=\#\{(p,\ell):p+\ell=n\}$ over $0\le p<P$, $0\le\ell<L$ is the
triangular anti-diagonal count of the plain Hankel lift
$[\mathcal Z]_{i,p,\ell,t}=[\bY_t]_{i,\,p+\ell}$. The implementation stores
the observation in that lifted form and solves
\eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} there, against the design whose $k$th column
is $\bd_{P,k}\otimes\bd_{L,k}$ with $[\bd_{P,k}]_p=z_k^{p}$ and
$[\bd_{L,k}]_\ell=z_k^{\ell}$, matching main-paper
Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:dk} up to the column-wise phase
$e^{j\pi(N-1)\Delta f\tau_k}$ that the factor coefficients absorb. Since
$\sum_{p,\ell}z_k^{p+\ell}\overline{z_l^{p+\ell}}
=\sum_n w_n z_k^n\bar z_l^n$, and identically for the correlation against
the data, the lifted normal equations and the weighted matrix ones of
\eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} are the same linear system, agreeing to
machine precision in the shipped code. Nothing is decomposed in the lifted
domain.

The coupled recovery \eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} uses the Tikhonov
loading $\lambda_\tau=\lambda_{\rm rel}
\tr(\bD_{\rm lift}^{\mathsf T}\bD_{\rm lift}^{\ast})/K+\lambda_{\rm fl}$,
where $\bD_{\rm lift}$ collects the $K$ lifted design columns above, with the
dimensionless relative term $\lambda_{\rm rel}=10^{-6}$ normalizing by that
design Gram and the absolute floor $\lambda_{\rm fl}=10^{-12}$. Neither
constant enters the reconstruction of the two frequency factors from the
shared pole, so neither can move a delay estimate.

\subsection{Beam-Space Acquisition}

The adjoint pass forms
$\tilde\bg_k=\ba_{{\rm RB},k}^{\ast}\odot(\bOmega_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k)$
and never inverts $\bOmega_k$. Trial ranges are $G_r=9$ points spaced
uniformly over the panel range bounds. At each of them the Fresnel dechirp
$e^{+j\kappa\|\brho_{k,m}\|^2/(2r)}$ is applied and a two-dimensional
transform zero-padded by a factor $4$ per axis, capped at $512$ bins per
axis, evaluates the correlation on the direction-cosine grid
$u=\lambda_c\nu_x/d_x$, $v=\lambda_c\nu_y/d_y$ at the transform frequencies
$\nu$. A bin is admissible when $u^2+v^2<1$ and when its normal cosine
$w=\sqrt{1-u^2-v^2}$ and azimuth fall inside the panel search bounds; the
phase-only response is front/back ambiguous, so only $w\ge0$ is enumerated.

Peaks are then taken on the range-maximized angular map
$\max_r S_r(u,v)$ rather than on the full cube, because the aperture
resolves direction far better than range. Four peaks are extracted greedily,
each followed by a guard of $\lceil 4/2\rceil=2$ bins per axis to prevent
re-selecting the same mainlobe, and every selected direction receives an
exact spherical range scan on $41$ uniformly spaced ranges before local
refinement. The retained fixed-count dictionary
$\mathcal C_k^{\rm cnt}$ is the $15\times9\times25$ range/elevation/azimuth
grid over the global panel bounds, and the local refinement grid is
$3\times3\times5$ in the same order over spans of $0.45$~m and $0.12$~rad
about the current iterate.

\subsection{Association}

The first tier evaluates all $K!$ permutations with the RIS term of
\eqref{main-eq:assoc_score} taken on the cached coarse codebook rather than
on the exact spherical projection, which is what makes the exhaustive
ranking affordable. The
\texttt{stage1\_assignment\_num\_exact\_permutations} setting fixes the
shortlist at two; the two best coarse permutations are then re-scored with
the exact spherical projection. The reported margin is the relative gap
$(C_{(2)}-C_{(1)})/C_{(1)}$ between the two exact totals, and a trial is
flagged unreliable when it falls below $0.3$.

\subsection{Phase~II Solvers and Tolerances}

The inner clock search seeds its incumbent from a zero-padded transform of
length $M_c=4096$ over $\mathcal T$, then runs interval branch-and-bound
with the second-derivative bound \eqref{main-eq:bound_short}, bisecting the
interval of largest upper bound. Subdivision stops when the global gap falls
below
\begin{equation}
	\epsilon_{\rm clk}=M\,\epsilon_{\rm abs}
	+\epsilon_{\rm rel}\max\{\|\by\|^2,|Q_{\rm best}|\},
	\label{eq:eps_clk_supp}
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon_{\rm abs}=10^{-12}$ and $\epsilon_{\rm rel}=10^{-10}$, or
when the cap $N_{\rm cap}=2\times10^4$ subdivisions is reached; the reported
gap remains a valid bound in either case. The outer position search is
L-BFGS-B~\cite{byrd1995lbfgsb} restricted to $\mathcal P$, with at most
$20$ iterations, $\texttt{ftol}=10^{-12}$ and $\texttt{gtol}=10^{-8}$, and a
Powell restart~\cite{powell1964direct} when a near-tied clock branch
makes the envelope derivative unreliable. The common-geometry stage runs
L-BFGS-B from its four deterministic starts under its own settings: at most
$30$ iterations, $\texttt{ftol}=10^{-10}$ and $\texttt{gtol}=10^{-6}$.
Those four starts are built in a fixed order and contain no random or oracle
state. The first is the clipped coordinate-wise median of the $K$ panel-local
fixes, $\bp_0=\mathrm{clip}_{\mathcal P}\{\mathrm{median}_k
\hat\bp_k^{\rm loc}\}$. The remaining budget is filled by taking at most one
of the axis-aligned displacements of $\bp_0$ by $\pm25$~cm, then the height
samples that replace the third coordinate of $\bp_0$ by a $7$-point grid
spanning $\mathcal P$ ordered outward from its centre, and only then the
remaining axial displacements;
every candidate is clipped back to $\mathcal P$ and duplicates are dropped,
so height diversity is reached before the axial sweep is exhausted.

The per-column confidence weights of main-paper
Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:Jgeom_short} use $\varrho_0=1/2$ and
$\varpi_{\min}=0.05$, so
a column whose rank-one split is poor is down-weighted smoothly while every
panel stays in the fit; $\varpi_{\min}>0$ is what prevents a single bad split
from removing a panel outright. In the anchor refresh, a block
$\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}$ whose norm is not finite or falls at or below
$10^{-12}$ carries no usable direction; the implementation then falls back to
the polarization direction already carried by the Stage-I estimate, and to
the fixed direction $[1,0]^{\mathsf T}$ if that too is unavailable. The
anchor is therefore always a unit vector and the directional penalty on path
$k$ is never switched off. This branch is never taken in the reported campaign.

The refreshed Jones anchor of \eqref{main-eq:refresh_short} is
$\hat\be_k=\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}/\|\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|_2$ whenever
$\|\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|_2$ exceeds the $10^{-12}$ threshold above, and the
fallback direction otherwise; either way $\|\hat\be_k\|_2=1$, so
$\bPi_{\hat\be_k}^{\perp}$ is a rank-one orthogonal projector and the penalty
weight of \eqref{main-eq:penalized_short} is unchanged by the branch.

\section{Complexity Accounting}
\label{sec:complexity_supp}

This section gives the term-by-term cost accounting summarized in
Section~\ref{main-sec:analysis}. The receive projection costs $O(IrNT)$ on
the matrices themselves; the implementation applies it to the stored
smoothed array instead, at $O(IrPLT)$, and pays $O(rTPLK+PLK^2+K^3)$ for
compressed factor recovery, the
last term being the $K\times K$ solve in
\eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short}. Beam-space acquisition requires one RIS
adjoint pass \eqref{main-eq:adjoint_short} plus $G_r$ two-dimensional FFTs
per panel, $G_r$ being the number of trial ranges, and common-geometry fusion
scales with its starts, iterations, panels, training blocks, and RIS
elements.

Each of the $I_p$ Phase~II outer position evaluations costs
\begin{align}
 C_{\rm eval}={}&O(KTM_R)+O\big(qM_r+q^2(N+T)\big)\nonumber\\
 &+O(q^2N^2+qM_c\log M_c+q^3)+C_{\rm cert},\nonumber\\
	&q=2K,\qquad M_r=rNT,
	\label{eq:complexity_supp}
\end{align}
with $M_c$ the zero-padded clock-FFT length. The $O(KTM_R)$ term generates
the exact near-field training responses and their position derivatives
using the dense $T\times M_R$ training matrices. The next bracket covers
the matched filter and the factorized Gram update in
\eqref{main-eq:gram_factorization}. The $O(q^2N^2)$ term assembles the $N$
coefficients of \eqref{main-eq:clock_coeff} from the $O(N^2)$ tone pairs,
each weighted through the $q\times q$ inverse Gram. The remaining terms
cover the clock FFT and the factorization of $\bG_\lambda$. The certificate term is
$C_{\rm cert}=O\big(N_{\rm int}(N+\log N_{\rm int})\big)$, since each of the
$N_{\rm int}\le N_{\rm cap}$ visited intervals costs one $O(N)$ evaluation of
the polynomial and its derivative plus one heap operation. The profiled solve
therefore acts on $M_r$ compressed rows instead of the $M=I\,N\,T$ raw rows,
and the exact spherical panel projection, not the certified clock profile,
dominates the measured runtime (Section~\ref{main-subsec:res_cost}).

\section{Certificate and Failure Diagnostics in Detail}
\label{sec:cert_supp}

Over the $4\,320$ operating-region trials of the internal SNR suite, all
$35\,696$ conditional clock profiles satisfy the prescribed tolerance; the
largest $g_{\rm cert}/\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ over all visited positions is
$0.999957$.

The median outer evaluation count is $8$, no Powell safeguard is selected,
and panel pairing is correct on all operating-region trials. Two trials
are catastrophic. A valid conditional certificate bounds clock
optimization error at the tested position; it does not bound statistical
estimation error or certify the outer position search.

The dedicated C3 control shares $100$ MKSC-GI initializers.
Its canonical proposed route performs $950$ clock profiles, all certified,
with a maximum $g_{\rm cert}/\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ ratio of $0.998024$.
These are the records summarized
in Table~\ref{main-tab:c3_control}.

\section{The Refinement-Matched Diagnostic Tier}
\label{sec:aspub}

The main comparison evaluates each adaptation at its native read-out.
The independently seeded refinement-matched suite additionally grants
VBI/SBL and Algebraic CPD an exact-model position--clock polish from their
own acquisition outputs. Comparisons within either stream are paired;
differences between the two streams also include realization differences.
Near-field OMP already uses its prescribed SAGE refinement and is not
changed by this tier.

At $-10$~dB, VBI/SBL's median changes from $26.1$~mm at native read-out to
$0.322$~mm in the matched suite, with catastrophic rates $9.90\%$ and
$0.83\%$. Within the matched stream the proposed route has $0.320$~mm
median and $0.42\%$ catastrophic events. The paired comparison rescues
eight VBI/SBL catastrophic trials and introduces four ($p=0.388$,
McNemar exact), so a rate difference is not established there.
The result supports the importance of the joint read-out for this
blocked-direct-path adaptation of~\cite{li2024vbi}.

Algebraic CPD's matched median is $0.664$~mm, but its catastrophic rate
remains $42.92\%$ ($46.67\%$ at native read-out). The matched comparison
against the proposed route has $408$ rescued and zero introduced
catastrophic trials. This places the persistent loss before the added
polish; Section~\ref{sec:als} distinguishes factor fitting, association
and the factor-to-position map.

