\section{Phase II Directional-Weight Ablation}
\label{sec:lambda_supp}

The fixed $\lambda_k=1$ does not satisfy the tapering condition of
Proposition~\ref{prop:compat}. We assess its finite-SNR effect using the
full-EVS arms of the archived weight sweep: four weights
$\{0,0.1,1,10\}$, five SNRs and $480$ shared-initializer trials per cell,
or $9\,600$ runs. No run fails. Each arm uses the same noisy observation
and Phase-I output; $\lambda_k=0$ removes the directional penalty, with
the common numerical loading retained.

Table~\ref{tab:s_lambda} reports conditional position and clock RMSE,
each conditioned on its own catastrophic-event complement as in the main
paper. The largest change over the weight range is
$6.23\times10^{-6}$~mm in position and $3.76\times10^{-5}$~ps in clock.
All weights produce the same catastrophic indicators in this full-EVS
sweep. The largest penalty/data-objective ratio is $6.45\times10^{-6}$.
Thus the penalty has little measured effect on these full-EVS results.

The reduced-receiver arms of the archived weight sweep are not used to
support this conclusion: their scalar high-SNR behavior has not been
reconciled with the corrected receiver-mode campaign.

