\section*{Part II: The Threshold Region Below $-10$~dB}

\section{Threshold Region: Mechanism}
\label{sec:thresh}

The main paper reports $\mathrm{SNR}\in[-10,30]$~dB; this part extends the
same protocols to $-15$ and $-20$~dB. In the matched compression stream,
median compressed subspace leakage $\sigma_{K+1}/\sigma_K$ rises from
$0.357$ at $-10$~dB to $0.574$ and $0.811$, while retained energy falls
from $0.148$ to $0.091$ and $0.072$, approaching the white-noise reference
$r/I=1/16$. These diagnostics indicate weakening delay-subspace separation.
They do not establish that every final error originates in Phase~I.

\section{Internal Structure Validation Below the Region}

Fig.~\ref{fig:s_internal} reports the independently seeded internal SNR
stream. The proposed catastrophic rate rises from $0.21\%$ at $-10$~dB
to $1.25\%$ (exact $95\%$ CI $[0.46,2.70]\%$) at $-15$~dB and
$5.63\%$ (CI $[3.74,8.08]\%$) at $-20$~dB; basin acquisition falls
from $99.79\%$ to $98.75\%$ and $93.75\%$.
The p95 Phase-I delay error grows from $0.110$ to $0.711$ and
$21.9$~ns, compared with medians of $0.025$, $0.049$ and $0.130$~ns.
Thus degradation is concentrated in the delay-error tail.

At $-20$~dB, R2 and R3 have catastrophic rates of $22.29\%$ and
$21.04\%$, versus $5.63\%$ for the proposed route and $5.42\%$ without
anchor refresh. Neither clock profiling nor refresh is guaranteed to
preserve the final event indicator. The matched-initializer C3 control,
not equality of these independently optimized routes, isolates profiling.
Against R2, the paired position-RMSE differences at $-15$ and $-20$~dB
are $-0.254$~m (bootstrap CI $[-0.349,-0.167]$) and $-0.334$~m
($[-0.408,-0.261]$). The corresponding rescued/introduced outlier counts
are $38/5$ and $91/11$ (exact McNemar $p=2.5\times10^{-7}$ and
$8.0\times10^{-17}$). These counts quantify a net reliability benefit,
not a per-trial improvement guarantee.

