\section*{Part III: Auxiliary Derivations and Extended Proofs}

\section{Local Identifiability with Unknown Jones Nuisance}
\label{sec:ident_proof_supp}

Let $(\bchi_\star,\bx_\star)$ be the true parameters, let
$\bD_\chi=[\partial(\bA\bx_\star)/\partial\chi_i]_{i=1}^4$, and define
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde{\bD}_\chi=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \Real\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}\\
 \Imag\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}
 \end{bmatrix},\qquad
 \bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}=\bI-\bA_\star\bA_\star^\dagger.
 \label{eq:s_ident}
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Local position--clock identifiability]
Suppose $\bA_\star$ has constant rank near the truth, the paths are
resolvable in the raw OFDM domain, the panel assignment is fixed, the truth
is interior, and $\rank_{\R}(\widetilde{\bD}_\chi)=4$. Then $\bchi_\star$
is first-order locally identifiable after eliminating the complex Jones
coefficients. Under white Gaussian noise this rank condition is equivalent
to positive definiteness of the free-Jones EFIM.
\end{proposition}

\begin{IEEEproof}
A first-order perturbation is
$\delta\bmu=\bD_\chi\,\delta\bchi+\bA_\star\delta\bx$ with
$\delta\bchi\in\R^4$. A nonzero
geometric direction can be canceled by a Jones perturbation exactly when
$\bD_\chi\,\delta\bchi\in\coll(\bA_\star)$, or equivalently
$\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\,\delta\bchi=\mathbf0$. Realifying this map gives
\eqref{eq:s_ident}, whose nullspace is trivial precisely at real rank four.
Its Gram matrix is
$\Real\{\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}$, proportional
to the free-Jones EFIM, proving the equivalence.
\end{IEEEproof}

The statement is local and conditional. In particular, three panels are
not sufficient by equation counting alone: the RIS codes must preserve the
near-field spatial tangents, the curvature must be nondegenerate, and the
panel geometry must avoid collinearity and symmetry. Global permutations
and finite-bandwidth ambiguities are not excluded.

\section{Nested-Component Information Order}
\label{sec:split_proof_supp}

Theorem~\ref{main-thm:split} and its proof, including the Kronecker
projector splitting lemma on which both parts rest, are in the main paper.
A numerical audit and two remarks extend them.

The single-panel scalar identity follows from the calibrated
$\partial\bTheta/\partial\bchi=0$ model. The receiver-mode plots evaluate
the full multi-panel estimator, for which the additional qualifications
below are needed.

First, the exact single-panel ratio
$\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta\bx\|^2/\|\bTheta\bx\|^2$
depends on $k$ once several panels are resolved, so the weighted sum over
panels is in general not a global rescaling of the aggregate EFIM. The main
paper states the block-orthogonal approximation this admits; nothing beyond
the single-panel identity is claimed exactly.

Second, for \emph{nested} component sets the free-Jones information order
follows without any assumption on the Jones block, from the variational
identity
\begin{equation}
 \bu^{\mathsf T}\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\bu
 =\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\min_{\delta\bx}
 \|\bD_\chi\bu+\bA\delta\bx\|^2 .
\end{equation}
Observing additional components appends nonnegative residual terms to the
minimized quadratic form, which therefore cannot decrease. This is why the
${\rm PEB}_{\rm scalar}\ge{\rm PEB}_{\rm dual}\ge{\rm PEB}_{\rm full}$
ordering of the reported bounds holds
even where a mask makes the retained Jones block singular and the exact
ratio $\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$ is not by itself enough to order the
aggregate.

\section{Spatial-Wideband Mismatch Diagnostic}
\label{sec:wideband_supp}

Section~\ref{sec:squint_supp} records that every released campaign generates
and estimates on the same center-frequency spatial manifold, so the neglected
frequency dependence contributes exactly zero residual there. This section
measures it directly instead. The observation is generated with a
per-subcarrier manifold, $f_n=f_c+\nu_n\Delta f$ and $\lambda_n=c_0/f_n$ at
the archived centered indices, and processed by the unchanged center-frequency
estimator. Panel geometry, element coordinates and the physical ULA spacing
are fixed across tones; the RIS codes, Jones states and gains are one draw
shared by all tones; and the center-path baseband delay phase is unchanged, so
the spatial responses add only element-relative path differences. In the
reference configuration the mismatch carries $0.13\%$ of the signal energy,
of which $7.5$~parts per million leave the calibrated receive subspace.

Two estimates are reported per sample. The first-order prediction solves
$\min_{\Delta\bz\in\R^4}\|\bP\boldsymbol{\delta}-\bP\bD_{\bz}\Delta\bz\|^2$ in the real
parameters, with $\boldsymbol{\delta}=[\bA_{\rm wb}-\bA_c]\bx^\star$,
$\bP=\bI-\bA_c\bA_c^{+}$, $\bz=[\bp^{\mathsf T},c_0\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}$ and
$\bx^\star$ constructed from the generator rather than fitted. The nonlinear
value is a noiseless local refinement from the true position. Both are free of
the directional penalty; a run with the paper penalty changes them by at most
$0.6\%$. One anchor control produced no reportable difference: replacing the
true Jones states by a Stage-I anchor carrying a $6.1\times10^{-3}$~rad error
left the result unchanged to every digit. That is a single control at a
single sample.

